  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Flash flood potential remains elevated through \nlate tonight along with marginal risk for strong to severe \nstorms. \n\nAn anomalous slow moving upper level low centered over the lower\nOhio Valley will continue to plague the forecast area with broad\nand impressive synoptic ascent impinging into an abundance of\nmoisture laden air over the eastern CONUS. Morning 12Z U/A \nregional soundings measured PWATs from 1.8 to 2.2 inches, well \nabove the 90th percentile, and record setting for the date here\nat RNK. This environment is ripe for for heavy rainfall within \nany convection which develops yielding off the chart kind of\nrainfall rates. Forecasters working the Mount Airy Emergency\nfrom last night said radar was estimating rainfall rates in \nexcess of 5-7 inches an hour. Within 3 hours time between 4-8 \ninches of rain fell within the headwaters of the Ararat River \nbasin resulting in Major Flooding in Mt. Airy NC. Extensive \nproperty and road damage was observed in addition to swift water\nrescues.\n\nConcern through this evening and into the overnight tonight is \nredevelopment of these rain efficient showers and \nthunderstorms. Thunderstorm clusters are expected to develop \nwithin an environment supported by moderate instability, surface\ndewpoints in the 70s, and PWATs near 2 inches, suggesting main \nthreat from any storm activity that develops will be from the \npotential for excessive rainfall. Although lapse rates will \nremain poor and vertical shear weak, water loading may lead to \nisolated strong wind gusts. Increasing directional shear around \nthe upper low may also lead to weak rotation...so an isolated \ntornado can't totally be ruled out either, esp for our \nneighboring WV counties closer to the Upper Low. \n\nFor now will continue to highlight a very broad area for the\nFlood Watch. Unlike yesterday when CAMs were highlighting the\nsouthern Blue Ridge. This evening the convergence seems to be\naligning with back edge of cloud field over the western CWA\nwhere dry slot around the upper low has allowed for some\nclearing and resultant surface heating and increasing CAPE.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues through much of the\nweek.\n \nThe closed low is forecast to gradually settle southeast over\nthe southern Appalachians Monday into Tuesday before weakening\nand filling mid week. Models maintain continued opportunity \nfor showers and thunderstorms with marginal risk of excessive\nrainfall. After mid-week, we still have daily afternoon and \nevening thundershower chances, but no real change in airmass. In\ngeneral weather will stay moist with surface dewpoints flirting\nwith 70.",
